<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <meta name="author" content="{{ contact.name }}">
  <title>{% block title %}{{ contact.name }} · Resume{% endblock %}</title>
  {% block head_extra %}{% endblock %}
  {% block fonts %}{% endblock %}
  <style>
    /* ---- Reset ---- */
    *, *::before, *::after { box-sizing: border-box; margin: 0; padding: 0; }
    /* ---- CSS Variables (override in child templates) ---- */
    :root {
      --accent: #1a56db;
      --text: #111827;
      --muted: #6b7280;
      --border: #e5e7eb;
      --bg: #ffffff;
      --font-body: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;
      --font-mono: "Courier New", monospace;
      --page-width: 210mm;
      --page-padding: 18mm 16mm;
    }
    {% block css_vars %}{% endblock %}

    /* ---- Base styles ---- */
    body {
      /* Kerning off, deliberately, and this is not a typographic preference.

         WeasyPrint emits a kerned pair as two positioned runs, and extractors read
         a large enough horizontal jump as a word break — so kerning puts spaces
         *inside* words in the text layer. Measured on Liberation Serif and Sans:
         "PAYPAL" came out "P AYP AL", "AVATAR" as "A V ATAR", "WAVE" as "WA VE".
         The page looks perfect; the words a recruiter searches for do not exist in
         the document. Any employer or skill with a hard kern pair — PA, AV, AW, Ta,
         Wa, Vo — is exposed, which is a lot of real names.

         The cost is marginally looser letter fitting at 10pt, which nobody will
         notice. The benefit is that every word in the CV is one word. */
      font-kerning: none;
      font-family: var(--font-body);
      font-size: 10pt;
      line-height: 1.35;
      color: var(--text);
      background: var(--bg);
      max-width: var(--page-width);
      margin: 0 auto;
      padding: 12mm 14mm;
    }
    h1 { font-size: 20pt; }
    h2 { font-size: 12pt; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .08em; color: var(--accent); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 2px; margin: 14px 0 6px; }
    h3 { font-size: 10pt; font-weight: 600; }
    a { color: var(--accent); text-decoration: none; }
    /* Header links carry the surrounding header's colour, so anchoring them
       does not restyle a design that puts the header on an accent background.
       `.contact-line` is the shared header container; a template with a container
       of its own — `cv/sidebar-compact` and its `.contact-list` — states the rule
       itself rather than teaching base one more name. */
    .contact-line a { color: inherit; }
    ul { padding-left: 1.2em; }
    li { margin-bottom: 2px; }
    p { margin-bottom: 4px; }

    /* ---- Print ---- */
    @page { size: A4; margin: var(--page-padding); }
    @media print {
      body { padding: 0; max-width: none; }
      a { color: inherit; }
    }

    /* ---- Pagination ----
       Every template is single-page by layout but not by content: a senior CV
       routinely runs to two or three. These rules are what make page two look
       deliberate rather than like the first page overflowed. A heading must not
       be the last thing on a page, and a job must not be split from its own
       bullets. Both properties are set: WeasyPrint honours the modern `break-*`
       names, `page-break-*` covers older renderers reading the same HTML.

       These rules reach a template through `.entry`, which is a *role*: "this box
       is one CV entry, keep it whole". A template's own class is its skin and
       carries no guarantee of its own — `cv/timeline-clean` writes
       `class="entry timeline-entry"` for exactly this reason. Base used to name the
       skin instead, which meant page-break protection was conditional on base
       having heard of your class name. `.section-title` is gone from the heading
       rule for a duller reason: it is only ever on an `<h2>`, already listed. */
    h2, h3 { break-after: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; }
    .entry { break-inside: avoid; page-break-inside: avoid; }
    li { break-inside: avoid; page-break-inside: avoid; }
    /* An entry longer than a page must still break, or it is dropped entirely. */
    .entry { orphans: 2; widows: 2; }

    /* ---- Entry heading and meta line ----
       The date runs inline on the meta line rather than in a right-hand column.
       A right-aligned date leaves an empty band down the page that geometric
       extractors read as a column and lift the dates out of.
       See docs/dev/architecture.md. */
    h3.entry-title { font-size: inherit; font-weight: 700; }
    /* One size and one family across the meta line: the company, the date and the
       location are peers on the same row, so only weight and colour distinguish
       them. Templates override `.entry-meta`, never the individual fields. */
    .entry-meta { color: var(--muted); }
    .entry-meta .entry-date, .entry-meta .entry-location { font-size: inherit; font-family: inherit; }
    .entry-sep { color: var(--muted); }

    /* ---- Skill level bars (skill_level_bar filter) ----
       The one place base styles a class only one packaged template shows: nothing
       writes `skill-level` in markup, the `skill_level_bar` filter emits it. This
       CSS is the rendering half of that filter's output, so it belongs to whoever
       owns the filter — any template calling it gets a bar without restyling one. */
    .skill-level { display: inline-block; width: 40px; height: 5px; background: var(--border); border-radius: 3px; position: relative; vertical-align: middle; margin-left: 4px; }
    .skill-level::after { content: ""; position: absolute; left: 0; top: 0; height: 100%; background: var(--accent); border-radius: 3px; }
    .skill-level-0 { display: none; }
    .skill-level-1::after { width: 25%; }
    .skill-level-2::after { width: 50%; }
    .skill-level-3::after { width: 75%; }
    .skill-level-4::after { width: 100%; }

    {% block css_extra %}{% endblock %}
  </style>
</head>
<body>
{% block body %}{% endblock %}
</body>
</html>
